{{/* @dot donseba/go-webthings-docs/internal/website.DocsArticleData */}}
<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<pre><code>go get github.com/donseba/go-translator/v2@v2.0.0</code></pre>
<p>Use the <code>/v2</code> import path; the package name stays <code>translator</code>. These examples use v2.0.0. Coming from v1, read the <a href="https://github.com/donseba/go-translator/tree/v2.0.0#upgrading">upgrading notes</a> first: <code>Tn</code> and <code>Ctn</code> changed signature, and some behaviour changed.</p>
<p>Create <code>translations/</code> and <code>templates/</code>, then save this as <code>translations/en_US.po</code>:</p>
<pre><code>msgid ""
msgstr ""
"Language: en_US\n"
"Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8\n"
"Plural-Forms: nplurals=2; plural=(n != 1);\n"

msgid "Hello, World!"
msgstr "Hello, World!"</code></pre>
<p>A translator made with <code>NewTranslator</code> appends every non-empty key a lookup uses to <code>translations/translations.pot</code> when it is not there yet. It creates that file with a gettext header when it does not exist yet; the translations directory must exist.</p>
<h2>A complete program</h2>
<pre><code>package main

import (
	"html/template"
	"log"
	"os"

	"github.com/donseba/go-translator/v2"
)

type Locale string

func (l Locale) GetLocale() string { return string(l) }

func main() {
	tr := translator.NewTranslator("translations", "templates")
	if err := tr.SetLanguage("en_US"); err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	page := template.Must(template.New("welcome").Funcs(tr.FuncMap()).Parse(
		`&lt;h1&gt;{{"{{"}} tl .Loc "Hello, World!" }}&lt;/h1&gt;`,
	))
	if err := page.Execute(os.Stdout, struct{ Loc Locale }{Loc: "en_US"}); err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
}</code></pre>
<p>The program prints a translated heading. Locale codes are catalogue names: <code>en_US</code> loads <code>en_US.po</code>, and by default a lookup for <code>en_US</code> only uses that catalogue (see <a href="{{ basePath }}/locales" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/locales" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">language fallback</a>). A catalogue without a <code>Language</code> header is loaded under the name it was loaded as.</p>
<h2>A translator with options</h2>
<pre><code>tr := translator.New(
	translator.WithTranslationsDir("translations"),
	translator.WithUntranslated(translator.ShowOriginal),
	translator.WithLanguageFallback(true),
)
if err := tr.LoadLanguages(os.DirFS("translations")); err != nil {
	log.Fatal(err)
}</code></pre>
<p><code>New</code> configures a translator in one call; each option does what the setter of the same name does. Unlike <code>NewTranslator</code>, it records nothing while translating unless you call <code>SetRecordMissing(true)</code>, so serving requests never writes to disk. The <a href="{{ basePath }}/api" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/api" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">API reference</a> lists the options.</p>
<p>The <a href="{{ basePath }}/extraction" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/extraction" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">extraction workflow</a> shows how to discover literal template keys before rendering and merge them into your language catalogues. The <a href="{{ basePath }}/extract" hx-get="{{ basePath }}/extract" hx-target="#content" hx-push-url="true">extract package</a> writes the whole POT file and checks what each language still misses.</p>
</article>
